1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,974
My friend repairs electronics and resells them.

2
00:00:02,974 --> 00:00:08,882
Finding his next purchase means checking Facebook Marketplace, eBay, and more, again and again.

3
00:00:08,882 --> 00:00:11,700
I built FlipCheck to give him one place to start.

4
00:00:12,675 --> 00:00:16,153
Save searches for a Legion, a MacBook, or a camera.

5
00:00:16,153 --> 00:00:19,833
Each can have its own budget, margin target, and costs.

6
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:24,090
Here, I'm preparing a laptop search alongside the existing camera search.

7
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:29,766
These are real listings already discovered from eBay Australia and Facebook Marketplace.

8
00:00:29,766 --> 00:00:36,535
Switch views, scroll through seller photos, and compare asking prices without starting from a different tab every time.

9
00:00:37,508 --> 00:00:42,107
For Facebook, choose your location or a suburb, then set a pickup radius.

10
00:00:42,107 --> 00:00:45,998
A cheap device across town can be less attractive once you allow for the trip.

11
00:00:46,967 --> 00:00:50,021
Open a listing and browse its actual seller photos.

12
00:00:50,021 --> 00:00:54,820
Local Gemma extracts the stated specifications and keeps the supporting text beside them.

13
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:58,193
Notice the lens specification beside its source excerpt.

14
00:00:58,193 --> 00:01:00,976
Unsupported details stay marked as unknown.

15
00:01:00,976 --> 00:01:04,808
You can inspect the evidence and correct details before making a decision.

16
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:08,217
FlipCheck also shows what is missing.

17
00:01:08,217 --> 00:01:10,835
Asking prices are not completed sales.

18
00:01:10,835 --> 00:01:16,766
A verified opportunity needs five matching sold comparisons, confirmed details, and known costs.

19
00:01:16,766 --> 00:01:20,434
Right now, genuine sold comparisons must be imported.

20
00:01:21,425 --> 00:01:23,583
Gemma runs locally through Ollama.

21
00:01:23,583 --> 00:01:28,335
I can change the prompts and repeat analysis without a hosted model bill for every listing.

22
00:01:28,335 --> 00:01:31,071
Discovery still uses online services.

23
00:01:31,071 --> 00:01:34,726
The model, records, and corrections stay on my machine.

24
00:01:35,717 --> 00:01:39,950
My friend liked it and said he would pay for a more complete version suited to his work.

25
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:41,507
He hasn't paid yet.

26
00:01:41,507 --> 00:01:46,200
Next: a dedicated repair and parts workflow, and a real sourcing trial.

27
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,285
FlipCheck.

28
00:01:47,285 --> 00:01:48,511
Less searching.

29
00:01:48,511 --> 00:01:50,092
More time for the work.
